ed","current_statement_sha256":"sha256:5c506e58cd62661ef461797302e6665bd6b56845009469c9bf71d3c627f2fda2","file":"preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","line":87,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"5030d8f179f5f83f4fda4b5f3f75eeb69513cee35824290dee5d1ebd53c2d522","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:5c506e58cd62661ef461797302e6665bd6b56845009469c9bf71d3c627f2fda2","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex#L87"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\nAssume the continuum hypothesis. There is an abstract elementary class\n\\(K\\) in a countable finitary relational language such that\n\\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}]\n \\item \\(\\LS(K)=\\aleph_0\\);\n \\item \\(K\\) has at least two nonisomorphic models of cardinality\n       \\(\\beth_{\\omega_2}=H(K)\\);\n \\item putting \\(\\Lambda=\\beth_{(2^{\\aleph_1})^+}\\),\n       the class \\(K\\) is categorical in every cardinal\n       \\(\\mu\\ge\\Lambda\\).\n\\end{enumerate}\nIn particular, \\(\\lambda=\\Lambda^+>H(K)\\) is a categoricity cardinal\nfrom which downward transfer to \\(H(K)\\) fails."}
